The suit that protects workers from dangerous electricity

Some of our favorite startup innovations are those that make workers safer:=
 for example, the robot that tamps down grain (https://thehustle.co/news/th=
e-bot-keeping-humans-out-of-dangerous-silos?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-9O08mguK3NFFoV=
-bTqPgkD5RvF9Oys9oQ1hZPy4uwjttdia8KML_7p_PqqfmPXGaL2BTlLZyu_CyUlxY9YyHmE_KQ=
XEw&_hsmi=3D400923625 ) in silos, or the one that paints lines (https://the=
hustle.co/newsletters/road-painting-is-dangerous-but-this-robot-can-do-it?_=
hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-9O08mguK3NFFoV-bTqPgkD5RvF9Oys9oQ1hZPy4uwjttdia8KML_7p_Pqqf=
mPXGaL2BTlLZyu_CyUlxY9YyHmE_KQXEw&_hsmi=3D400923625 ) on roads. They offer =
a practical solution to some of the most dangerous, yet necessary work huma=
ns perform.

Power-line work is one of those risky jobs. Though the industry adheres to =
practices meant to safeguard workers from dangerous currents, fatal acciden=
ts can still occur.

One of the biggest hazards is induction:

- That=E2=80=99s when a worker comes into contact with a line or piece of e=
quipment that isn=E2=80=99t supposed to be energized but has become so due =
to nearby electric or magnetic fields, resulting in shocks, injury, or deat=
h.

- There were 81 induction accidents (https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/9=
925039?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-9O08mguK3NFFoV-bTqPgkD5RvF9Oys9oQ1hZPy4uwjttdia8KML=
_7p_PqqfmPXGaL2BTlLZyu_CyUlxY9YyHmE_KQXEw&_hsmi=3D400923625 ) in the US bet=
ween 1985 and 2021, resulting in 60 deaths, per US Bureau of Labor Statisti=
cs data.

But Eduardo Ramirez Bettoni, who helped develop a suit that protects worker=
s and who now works for a power-distribution equipment firm, told (https://=
spectrum.ieee.org/transmission-line-safety-suit?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-9O08mguK3N=
FFoV-bTqPgkD5RvF9Oys9oQ1hZPy4uwjttdia8KML_7p_PqqfmPXGaL2BTlLZyu_CyUlxY9YyHm=
E_KQXEw&_hsmi=3D400923625 ) IEEE Spectrum that he believes there are hundre=
ds of induction contacts every year in the US alone.

He also suspects incidents are increasing as grid operators attempt to boos=
t capacity.

About this suit=E2=80=A6

It comes from Electrostatics (https://elstatics.com/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-9O08m=
guK3NFFoV-bTqPgkD5RvF9Oys9oQ1hZPy4uwjttdia8KML_7p_PqqfmPXGaL2BTlLZyu_CyUlxY=
9YyHmE_KQXEw&_hsmi=3D400923625 ) , a Budapest startup that makes jumpsuits =
(complete with gloves and socks) to protect workers.

How they work:

- The suits are made with conductive materials, but also have low-resistanc=
e conductive straps through which current can flow out.

- They=E2=80=99re flame retardant and insulated to protect against heat.

- They keep exposure below the =E2=80=9Clet-go=E2=80=9D threshold (https://=
www.pat-testing-training.net/articles/electric-shock.php?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-9=
O08mguK3NFFoV-bTqPgkD5RvF9Oys9oQ1hZPy4uwjttdia8KML_7p_PqqfmPXGaL2BTlLZyu_Cy=
UlxY9YyHmE_KQXEw&_hsmi=3D400923625 ) =E2=80=94 when a current is so strong,=
 it causes the muscles to seize involuntarily and prevents someone from rel=
easing themselves.

Electrostatics began selling the suits in 2023, and now provides them to tr=
ansmission operators across the US, Europe, Canada, and elsewhere. In the U=
S, they cost ~$5.2k per suit.

A small price to pay for safety =E2=80=94 and not as expensive as other pro=
tective gear. A firefighter=E2=80=99s full outfit, for example, can run bet=
ween (https://meyersvillefire.org/%F0%9D%97%A7%F0%9D%97%B5%F0%9D%97%B2-%F0%=
9D%97%A7%F0%9D%97%BF%F0%9D%98%82%F0%9D%97%B2-%F0%9D%97%96%F0%9D%97%BC%F0%9D=
%98%80%F0%9D%98%81-%F0%9D%97%BC%F0%9D%97%B3-%F0%9D%97%A2%F0%9D%98%82%F0%9D%=
98%81/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-9O08mguK3NFFoV-bTqPgkD5RvF9Oys9oQ1hZPy4uwjttdia8KML=
_7p_PqqfmPXGaL2BTlLZyu_CyUlxY9YyHmE_KQXEw&_hsmi=3D400923625#:~:text=3DProte=
cting%20lives%20isn't%20cheap,NextANNUAL%20FISH%20FRY ) $12.8k and $18.6k.

